## Lore

The Angakoq is the shaman of the Inuit, the people of the Tundra and the
Arctic. Nobody trains for the role. The spirits choose: an ordinary Inuit has
a prophetic dream that tests compassion and ease among spirits, a helper
animal comes forward, and whoever welcomes it wakes as a Shaman. The gifts
show themselves within a day and using them comes as second nature. Most are
chosen between fifteen and thirty, and about seven in ten are men.

Blessed by the spirit of the Polar Bear, an Angakoq knows animals and the
spirit world, recognises the supernatural on sight, and speaks with spirits
and demons as comfortably as with people, seeking advice, wisdom and omens.
They work magic, call on animal spirits through amulets and talismans, and
are prized above all for what they know and for their healing.

Within three days of the calling the Shaman is joined by the Tornaq, a spirit
guardian in the form of a bear sent by the Bear God Tornarssuk. It hunts,
tracks and fights for its partner, and its store of mystic energy is the
source of the Shaman''s greatest magic. The two protect each other; a Shaman
who lets the Tornaq die loses that magic for good and lives on in disgrace.

Angakoq are held in high regard by the Inuit, by most Native Americans, and
by the Mastadonoids and Tundra Rangers, and are made welcome wherever they
are known.

## GM Notes

The spell list on the sheet holds two groups. The Angakoq''s own thirteen
spells (ability 5) are cast from personal P.P.E. Everything else is a Tornaq
spell: cast by the Shaman from the bear''s reserve of 1D4x100+150 P.P.E., each
at most twice per 24 hours, and all lost if the bear dies. The Shaman can draw
up to half the bear''s P.P.E.

The book prints no I.S.P. figure and no psionic tier for the class; the eight
powers are granted outright. Settle I.S.P. before play.

Starting kit beyond what the sheet lists: a variety of arrows for the bow,
possibly a light suit of M.D.C. body armor made from Mega-Damage creatures, a
pick axe, cooking utensils, a heavy fur coat with hood, mittens, snowshoes and
a handful of personal items.

Money: no coin. Starts with 2D4x100 credits worth of herbs, furs, horns, bone,
nuggets of silver or gold and other trade goods. Those who know the Shaman
supply food, shelter and gifts in return for services and advice. Enchanted
amulets are treated as priceless.

Amulets and talismans (printed 185-186): two of each to start. Each new amulet
permanently costs the Tornaq 10 P.P.E. and each talisman or special item 20.
The Bear''s Coat, Demon Mask and Spirit Catcher are special items made in play.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'inuit-shaman');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'inuit-shaman';

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
-- footer, or whose footer names a different file.
INSERT INTO data_script_runs (filename) VALUES ('add-inuit-shaman-class.sql');
